1 Getting started

1.1 What is M-Commander?

   M-Commander is a user-friendly yet powerful file manager and visual
   shell, useful to novice and guru alike.  It provides a clear,
   user-friendly, and somewhat protected interface to a Unix system
   while making many frequent file operations more efficient and
   preserving the full power of the command prompt.  After some
   practice, you will wonder how you could ever live without it.

   The command is called mcommander, with mc6 as a short alias for it.
   The editor, the viewer and the diff viewer are mcedit6, mview and
   mcdiff6, and the tree and structure views keep the names mctree and
   mcstruct.  The package is called mcommander, the repository and the
   configuration directories mc6.

1.2 How does it relate to GNU Midnight Commander?

   M-Commander is a fork of GNU Midnight Commander 4.8.33.  It is not
   an official GNU package, and the GNU project does not maintain it.

   It keeps the keys, the layout and the file formats of the program it
   comes from, and adds panel plugins, an embedded terminal, an archive
   manager and a good deal more; the README file lists the additions.

   Report problems with this fork here, not to the upstream project,
   and do not report problems of the original program to us.

1.3 Does it run on my machine?

   M-Commander runs on POSIX (Unix-like) operating systems, such as
   GNU/Linux, FreeBSD, macOS, Solaris and others.

   It uses GNU Autoconf, which should configure it for every Unix
   clone.  The packages built for each release cover Debian, Ubuntu,
   Fedora, RHEL and Arch.

1.4 Does it work with my terminal?

   Yes, it does.

   Because this is a full screen program it doesn't run on dummy
   terminals, but anything more advanced will do (like vt100).  If your
   terminal works with vi, emacs, elm or pine it will work here.

1.5 What else do I need to run it?

   You need a POSIX (Unix compatible) operating system and glib >= 2.58,
   available at https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib/ .

   If you want to use the mouse on the Linux console you need the gpm
   daemon from https://www.nico.schottelius.org/software/gpm/ .  You
   need nothing extra to use the mouse on xterm.

   The screen is drawn through the S-Lang library or through ncurses;
   "mc -V" says which one this build uses.  Panel plugins need GModule,
   which comes with glib.  Everything else is optional and is listed in
   the INSTALL file.

1.6 Is M-Commander Public Domain? Copyrighted?

   It is under the GNU General Public License, which basically means
   that you may freely copy, change and distribute it, but that you may
   not impose any restrictions on further distribution, and that you
   must make the source code available.  This is not the same as Public
   Domain.  The license is included in the source distribution (the
   COPYING file).

   The code that comes from GNU Midnight Commander belongs to the Free
   Software Foundation, to which its authors gave their rights.  The
   code written for the fork belongs to its own authors, listed in the
   AUTHORS file.

1.7 Where can I get M-Commander?

   The source and the packages of every release are at

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander/releases

   and the development version is in the master branch of

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander

1.8 I cannot compile it.  What should I do?

   Make sure you have read the INSTALL file in the sources.  Report the
   error messages exactly as they appear, mention the versions of your
   OS, your compiler and whatever else software you think is relevant.
   If a panel plugin is what fails to build, leave it out with
   "./configure --enable-panel-plugin-<name>=no"; "./configure --help"
   lists the names.


2 Keyboard

2.1 What does documentation mean with the C-?, M-? and F? keys?

   The documentation uses emacs style names for keyboard keys.

   C stands for the Ctrl key.  For example, C-f means that you should
   hold down the Ctrl key and press the f key.

   M stands for the Meta key.  Your terminal might call it Alt or
   Compose instead of Meta.  For example, M-f means that you should hold
   down the Meta/Alt/Compose key and press the f key.  If your terminal
   doesn't have Meta, Alt or Compose or they don't work you can use Esc.
   For M-f press the Esc key and then press the f key.

   Sometimes Ctrl and Alt are used instead of C and M for simplicity.
   Keep in mind that Alt can actually be Meta on some keyboards.

   F? stands for a function key.  If your terminal doesn't have function
   keys or they don't work you can use Esc.  For example, for F3 press
   the Esc key and then press the 3 key.

2.2 Why don't function keys (or some other key) work?

   Your terminfo or termcap database has missing or incorrect
   definitions for function keys.  Type "mc -V" to see what terminal
   database is being used.

   Up-to-date termcap and terminfo databases are available here:

     http://www.catb.org/~esr/terminfo/
     https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/ncurses/

   If you don't have permission to edit terminal databases, teach the
   program the key instead: Options -> Learn keys (Esc 9 o k), press the
   key and save.  What it learns is kept per terminal type in
   ~/.config/mc6/term/$TERM, so a key learned in one terminal does not
   disturb another.

   Options -> Key sniffer shows the bytes your terminal really sends,
   which is the fastest way to find out what is going on.

   If all else fails you can emulate function keys by first pressing the
   ESC key and then one of the number keys.  For example, if you want to
   produce F9, press ESC, then 9.  If you don't have an ESC key on your
   keyboard you can try alt-9 or meta-9.

2.3 How do I use function keys F11 to F20?

   These are normally mapped to function keys F1 to F10 with Shift held,
   e.g. function key F13 can be activated by pressing Shift-F3.  You can
   define the keys this way in the Options -> Learn keys dialog.  The
   convention for PC keyboards is that F11-20 always means Shift with
   F1-10.

   You may find that on the Linux console with some keyboard layouts
   Shift-Fn is interpreted as F(n+12), not as F(n+10).  This is a result
   of non-uniformity of keyboard layouts.  Use "Learn keys" to remedy
   this inconvenience.

2.4 Why does the ESC key behave funny?

   The ESC key is a prefix for simulating the Meta and Alt keys (for
   terminals which don't have Meta or Alt, see the three previous
   questions).  For example, pressing ESC-a is the same as pressing
   Meta-a.  In addition most terminals use ESC for internal
   representation of arrow keys, function keys and other enhanced keys.
   If you want to use ESC to cancel things you have to press it twice,
   i.e. ESC-ESC.  If you find this cumbersome you can generally use F10
   to cancel.  Alternatively turn on the old_esc_mode setting in the
   ~/.config/mc6/ini file.  The old_esc_mode setting makes ESC work as a
   prefix only if another key is pressed within 0.5 seconds.  After 0.5
   seconds the ESC key cancels.  There is no way to make ESC cancel
   immediately (if we want to be able to use arrow keys and function
   keys).

2.5 How can I add the plus sign (+) on the command line?

   Press C-q first, then press the + sign.

   The plus key is the hotkey for the select files command.  If you want
   to add a literal plus on to the command line you must quote it by
   pressing C-q first.

   Another common key which needs the C-q prefix is backslash "\".

2.6 C-o doesn't work!

   Maybe C-o is a stty control character on your terminal.  See man stty
   for details on how to list and change stty control characters.

2.7 What 'keys' are the "a1" and "c1" keys mentioned in the manual?

   A1 is the "Home" key on the keypad (numpad 7 with Num Lock turned
   off), which is decoded as a different key from the regular "Home".
   Likewise C1 is keypad "End", A3 is keypad "Page Up", C3 is keypad
   "Page Down", and B2 is documented to "center of keypad", i.e. the 5
   key, though I don't know whether any keyboards actually use that.
   They are documented for the curses library (search for "keypad"):

   https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/007908799/xcurses/curses.h.html

2.8 How do I change the key bindings?

   Options -> Key bindings lists every command of every part of the
   program with the keys it answers to, and rebinds them on the spot.
   What you change there is written to ~/.config/mc6/keymap.ini.

   The same file can be written by hand, and so can the system-wide
   /etc/mcommander/keymap.ini; the mcommander(1) man page describes the format.

2.9 What does my terminal really send for this key?

   Options -> Key sniffer shows the raw bytes of every key you press,
   together with the name the program gives it.  Use it when a key does
   nothing, or when two keys look the same to the program.


3 Mouse

3.1 How do I enable mouse support?

   Invoke mc like this (without quotes): "mc -x".  If this doesn't work
   upgrade to a terminal which is compatible with the Xterm mouse
   sequences.

   Alternatively, on the Linux console you can use gpm.

3.2 How do I cut and paste text with mouse?

   Hold down the shift key while using the mouse to cut and paste.


4 Display

4.1 Why do I keep getting "Terminal not powerful enough for SLang" or
    "Terminal not powerful enough for SLsmg"?

   This means that your terminfo databases do not contain the correct
   definitions for your terminal.

   You could try using a different terminal setting.  If you use csh or
   tcsh:

     setenv TERM vt100

   or if you use sh, bash, ksh or zsh:

     export TERM=vt100

4.2 Why don't line drawing characters work?

   The command line option -a forces the use of the characters +, |, -
   for line drawing.  Use it if none of the suggestions below helps.

   In general, there are three cases:
     * Lines are shown as ASCII characters like this

      +---------+
      |         |
      +---------+

       This also happens when you use the -a option.  Other than that
       possible reason is 1 or 2 (see below).

     * Lines are shown as lower case characters like this

      lqqqqqqqqqk
      x         x
      mqqqqqqqqqj

       Possible reason is 1 or 2 (see below).

     * Lines are shown as blanks or missing characters.  Possible reason
       is 2 or 3 (see below).

   The reason for the problem is one of following:

    1. Your terminal might not support line drawing characters.  VT100
       compatible terminals, rxvt and xterm and color_xterm do support
       them.
    2. Your terminfo or termcap database might have missing or incorrect
       definitions for line drawing characters.  Set the acsc variable
       in the terminfo database like this:
       acsc=a\376k\277l\332m\300j\331n\305w\302v\301u\264t\303q\304x
       \263h\2600\333
       Don't forget to issue the 'tic' command.  This supposes you are
       using the PC character set.  The octal values might be different
       for other character sets.  If you are using termcap instead of
       terminfo, you should modify the above solution appropriately.
    3. Your terminal font might not support line drawing characters.
       Try changing the font.

4.3 Can one use latin-1 characters without losing the lines?

   Yes, you need a correct font and a correct termcap/terminfo database.

   For font, if you use xterm try "xterm -fn fixed".

   For termcap/terminfo database, change the acsc capability in the
   database.

4.4 How can I get colors?

   Invoke mc like this (without quotes): "mc -c".

   If you get colors, be happy.

   If your terminal stays black and white, your terminal doesn't support
   color.  You might want to upgrade to a terminal which is compatible
   with the ANSI color sequences.

   If your terminal goes completely black, see the next question.

   More detailed answer:

   Check that your terminal supports color.  Most terminals do. You can
   test color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[32m Hello color world! \033[m\n"

   You can test 256 color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[38;5;120m Hello 256-color world! \033[m\n"

   You can test true color support with the following command:

        printf "\033[38;2;0;200;0m Hello true color world! \033[m\n"

   If you see the text in green, your terminal supports the respective
   color mode.

   Check that you are using the proper TERM variable for your terminal.
   If not, set it accordingly. You can use the 'toe -a' command to list
   all available terminfo entries.

   If your terminal supports 256 colors, the correct entry name may be
   appended with -256color. If your terminal supports true color, it
   should be appended with -direct, -direct16, or -direct256. All three
   variants provide both 256-color and true color support simultaneously.
   The latter two variants make no difference here, and they support
   basic colors including their bright versions along with true color
   support, in contrast to the -direct variant, which supports only basic
   8 colors together with true color when used in a skin.

   If there is no 256-color or true-color terminfo variant for your
   terminal, even though your terminal supports it, send an e-mail to
   ncurses/terminfo maintainers (bug-ncurses@gnu.org) and ask them to
   add one.

   With the S-Lang library (you can check by 'mc -V'), you can force
   color support by setting the environment variable COLORTERM to any
   value, and specifically force true color support by setting it to
   'truecolor'.

4.5 My color_xterm goes completely (or partially) black!

   Some color_xterm terminals define all colors as black instead of the
   standard ANSI colors.  This makes them go completely black when you
   try to use colors.

   You will have to override the defaults.  Create a file
   "color.defaults" which has the following contents:

    color_xterm*color0:           Black
    color_xterm*color1:           Red
    color_xterm*color2:           Green
    color_xterm*color3:           Yellow
    color_xterm*color4:           Blue
    color_xterm*color5:           Magenta
    color_xterm*color6:           Cyan
    color_xterm*color7:           White
    color_xterm*background:       White
    color_xterm*foreground:       Black

   (replace color_xterm with the name of your color_xterm, color_xterm
   mentions its name in its title bar)

   Now type:

    xrdb -merge color.defaults

   Alternatively you can add the suggested contents of the
   color.defaults file to your .Xdefaults or .Xresources file (or what
   ever the name of your X configuration file is).  Or you can replace
   your non-ANSI color_xterm with an ANSI color_xterm.

4.6 I got colors working here but the other programs don't work at all
anymore!

   This program uses the terminfo database (if available) but many other
   programs use the termcap database.  If you set the TERM environment
   variable to a value which has no corresponding entry in the termcap
   database those programs stop working.  You should add the new value
   of TERM to the termcap database.

   Example: If you have set TERM to xterm-color locate from /etc/termcap
   the line which starts:

    xterm|vs100|xterm terminal emulator

   Change it to start:

    xterm|xterm-color|vs100|xterm terminal emulator

4.7 How do I change the colors of the program itself?

   Colors come from a skin file.  Options -> Appearance lists the skins
   that are installed and applies one at once; the choice is kept in
   ~/.config/mc6/ini.

   System skins live in /usr/share/mcommander/skins, your own in
   ~/.local/share/mc6/skins.  A skin is a plain ini file, so copying one
   of the system skins into your own directory and editing it is the
   usual way to make your own.  The skineditor plugin does the same job
   in a dialog, with a preview.

4.8 Why is the program linked with X libraries?

   It reads key modifiers from the X server, which helps to tell apart
   keys that the terminal emulator reports in the same way, e.g. PgUp
   and Ctrl-PgUp.  The libraries are loaded dynamically on the systems
   that support it, and "mc -V" says whether this build has the support
   at all.


5 Command line and the terminal

5.1 How do I stay in the last directory when I exit?

   See the description of the -P option in the Options section of the
   manual.

5.2 How can I access command line history?

   Meta-h shows the last commands you executed from the command line.
   Duplicates are suppressed from the history.  Commands executed at the
   terminal's own prompt are not shown.

   You can put previous commands on the command line with Meta-p.
   Meta-n moves you forward in history.  All input widgets have a
   permanent history; M-h summons the history listbox in each of them.

5.3 How can I complete commands, file names, variable names and so on?

   Just press M-Tab.  Press M-Tab again to get a listbox if there are
   multiple possible completions.

5.4 Which shells are supported?

   Any shell runs in the terminal.  The integration that keeps the
   command line, the prompt and the directory in step with the shell
   works with bash, zsh and fish; ash, ksh, mksh and their relatives run
   without it.

   Startup files of your own for those shells live in
   ~/.local/share/mc6: bashrc, ashrc, kshrc, mkshrc and .zshrc, each
   falling back to the one in your home directory.

5.5 Is there any way to include additional options or hot keys?

   Yes, F2 invokes a user menu, which is fully configurable.  You can
   add any shell commands to it.  Unlike in the program this one comes
   from, the menu edits itself: F2 opens it, and its entries are added
   and changed in the dialog, which keeps them in
   ~/.config/mc6/menu.ini.  A menu file written by hand still works; see
   the mcommander(1) man page.

   Another way is a panel plugin: the panel can be filled by a program
   of your own.  See doc/PLUGINS in the source distribution.

   And finally, you can code any feature you want yourself.  The source
   code is free, which means you can change it any way you want.  There
   are some limitations to make sure it stays free.  See the GNU General
   Public License for details.

5.6 When I use Ctrl-O the command line does not follow my shell.  How
do I fix this?

   The command line reads its prompt and its directory from bash, zsh
   and fish through a small integration.  Use one of those shells as
   your default shell to get it.  Other shells still run in the
   terminal, only without that integration.

5.7 Ctrl-O doesn't work at all.  What happens?

   Ctrl-O shows the terminal that runs behind the panels, and Ctrl-O
   again brings the panels back.  The terminal starts in the directory
   of the current panel, and the panel follows the shell when you cd in
   it.

   If Ctrl-O does nothing, your terminal may be eating the key; see
   question 2.6.

   C-F1 and C-F2 hide the left and the right panel, so the terminal can
   have the whole screen while a panel is still in reach.

5.8 Why do I see lots of strange 'cd "printf ' lines in my .history
file?

  Add

    export HISTCONTROL="ignoreboth"

  into your ~/.profile file (.bash_profile) to avoid this.

5.9 I use Screen and the output of previous commands is blanked.

  Update GNU Screen to the last version.  This bug was fixed in

   commit ad56f746c6243d45124485d198d577bdbb78071c
   Author: Sadrul Habib Chowdhury <sadrul@users.sourceforge.net>
   Date:   Sun Nov 29 23:34:25 2009 -0500

       Fix using alternate screen buffers in some cases.

       Screen would reset the 'main' screen buffer if an app tries to
       switch to an alternate buffer while it is already using one (in
       other words, sends multiple 'smcup' without an 'rmcup'). This should
       fix debian #558724

  (see https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=558724)

  or do not rewrite TERM (must be TERM=screen) and run mc as follows:

   echo -e '\e[?1000h' && mc -x


6 Archives, remote hosts and virtual file systems

6.1 How can I see the contents of a tar or zip archive?

   Select the archive and press Enter.  The arcmc plugin opens it in the
   panel, and you walk it like a directory; F5 copies files out of it,
   and it can also create and extract archives.  It handles tar and its
   compressed forms, zip, jar, 7z and cpio through libarchive, and hands
   the formats libarchive does not know to external helpers.

   F3 on an archive shows what is inside it as text.

   The built-in tarfs and cpiofs of GNU Midnight Commander are gone,
   with them the utar:// and ucpio:// paths.

6.2 How do I get out of an archive?

   Press Enter on ".." as usual.  At the top of the archive this closes
   the plugin, and the panel goes back to the directory the archive is
   in.

6.3 How do I connect to FTP, SFTP or a Samba share?

   Each of them is a panel plugin.  Open the list with M-F1 for the left
   panel or M-F2 for the right one, or with Command -> Plugin panel, and
   pick FTP, SFTP, Samba, shell link (that is ssh), S3, MongoDB, docker,
   Kubernetes or systemd.

   The plugin asks for the host and the credentials, and keeps what you
   answer in ~/.config/mc6, one file per plugin, for example
   ftp-connections.ini or sftp-connections.ini.  From then on the
   connection is one Enter away in that list.

6.4 What happened to "cd ftp://host"?

   The built-in ftpfs and sftpfs are gone; the FTP and SFTP plugins
   replace them, and a path typed on the command line no longer opens a
   connection.  Use the plugin list from question 6.3.

   The upside is that a connection lives in a panel of its own and no
   longer freezes the whole program while it waits for the network.

6.5 Which virtual file systems are left?

   Two, both local:

     * extfs - a script per format, which lists an archive or another
       container and extracts single files from it.  This is what shows
       you the inside of an rpm, a deb, an iso9660 image, a mailbox or a
       git commit.  "mc -V" prints where the scripts are installed;
       copy one to ~/.local/share/mc6/extfs.d to change it.

     * sfs - a single command per format, run once, whose output becomes
       the panel.

   Everything that used to need a network VFS is a panel plugin now.


7 Plugins

7.1 What is a panel plugin?

   A shared library that fills a panel with something that is not a
   directory: a git repository, a docker host, a Kubernetes cluster, an
   S3 bucket, a MongoDB collection, an archive, a remote host over FTP,
   SFTP, Samba or ssh, the systemd units of the machine, the fields of a
   binary file.  The panel keeps its keys, its marks and its file
   operations; only the source of the rows is different.

   The plugins shipped with the program are listed in the README file;
   doc/PLUGINS in the source distribution describes how to write one.

7.2 How do I put a plugin in a panel?

   Command -> Plugin panel lists the plugins that are loaded and puts
   the one you pick in the current panel.  M-F1 and M-F2 open the same
   list for the left and the right panel, next to the drives.

   Some plugins are opened for you: Enter on an archive starts arcmc,
   and the rules that say so live in magic.ini.

7.3 How do I turn a plugin off, or configure it?

   Options -> Manage plugins lists every plugin that is installed, with
   a switch for each.  What you turn off there is not loaded at the next
   start; the state is kept in ~/.config/mc6/plugins.ini.

   A plugin that has settings of its own opens them from that same
   dialog with Enter or F4.  Its connections, if it has any, are in
   ~/.config/mc6 in a file of its own.

7.4 Where do plugins live?

   Panel plugins are in $libdir/mc/panel-plugins, one directory per
   plugin, and editor plugins in $libdir/mc/editor-plugins.  Your own
   go to ~/.local/lib/mc6/panel-plugins and
   ~/.local/lib/mc6/editor-plugins, and a plugin of yours takes
   precedence over a system plugin of the same name.

   "mc -V" prints both paths and what it found in them.

7.5 My plugin in ~/.local/lib/mc is not loaded any more

   That directory belonged to the older versions of the fork, when the
   user directories were still called mc.  It is not read any more.

   Do not move the files: a plugin is tied to the exact version it was
   built against, and one built for an older release will not load.
   Build it again, put the result in ~/.local/lib/mc6/panel-plugins,
   and remove the old directory by hand:

     rm -rf ~/.local/lib/mc/panel-plugins

7.6 What are editor plugins and Lua scripts?

   Editor plugins are the same idea inside mcedit6: a shared library that
   adds commands to the editor menu, for example the ctags source
   navigator.

   Lua scripts are smaller and need no compiler.  They live in
   /usr/share/mcommander/lua/scripts and in ~/.local/share/mc6/lua/scripts, and
   a script with the same name in your own directory shadows the system
   one.  doc/LUA_PLUGINS.md and doc/LUA_API_REFERENCE.md describe what
   they can do.


8 Editor and viewer

8.1 When I try pasting to the internal editor, the amount of indenting
increases with each line!

   Turn off 'Return does autoindent' in the editor options, or hold
   Shift while you paste, which suspends the autoindent.

8.2 How do I add syntax highlighting for my language?

   The rules are in /usr/share/mcommander/syntax, one file per language, and the
   file Syntax there says which of them a file name gets.  To add or
   change a rule, copy the syntax directory to
   ~/.local/share/mc6/syntax, edit your copy, and the editor takes it
   instead of the system one.

   The editor also has code folding, an undo history on Alt-Shift-u and
   a macro explorer on Alt-Shift-m; a filter on Alt-s hides every line
   that does not match, which is a quick way through a log.

8.3 The file is huge and I only need the lines that match

   F6 in the viewer asks for a pattern and then shows only the lines
   that match it, the way grep does, without loading the file into
   memory.  "[" and "]" step through the matches, Ctrl-t follows a file
   that is still growing, and F6 again takes the filter off.

8.4 Can the viewer show JSON or a binary structure?

   Alt-s (or "t") in the viewer shows JSON, YAML, XML and HTML as a
   tree, which you fold and unfold with Enter, "+" and "-".

   Shift-F4 hands the file to mcstruct, which reads a def-file and shows
   the file as a tree of named fields beside a hex dump; ELF, PE, ZIP,
   DBF, MBR, FAT, uImage, DTB, PNG, BMP and WAV are shipped.  Fields are
   edited in place.


9 Configuration files

9.1 Where does the program keep my settings?

   In three directories, following the XDG layout:

     ~/.config/mc6        ini, panels.ini, menu.ini, keymaps, plugin
                          settings and connections
     ~/.local/share/mc6   skins, syntax, extfs.d, Lua scripts, editor
                          files, shell startup files
     ~/.cache/mc6         Tree, and other data that can be thrown away

   Plugins of your own live in ~/.local/lib/mc6.  "mc -V" prints every
   one of these paths, which is the quickest way to see where a
   particular file went.

9.2 I upgraded from GNU Midnight Commander.  Where did my settings go?

   Nowhere: on the first start, if ~/.config/mc6 does not exist yet and
   ~/.config/mc does, it is copied over, and the same for the data and
   cache directories.  The program says so on stderr.

   The old directories are left where they are, so the original program
   keeps working with them.  Nothing is written back to them.


10 Other common problems

10.1 Is there any way to 'bookmark' favorite places?

   Use the directory hotlist.  Just press control-backslash.  If your
   national keyboard layout doesn't have a backslash key, just press the
   control key with the key which is the backslash key in the English
   keyboard layout.

   A connection of a panel plugin is remembered by the plugin itself,
   see question 7.3.

10.2 When I start the program, nothing happens!

   Check the shell you are using.  The command line integration works
   best with bash, zsh and fish.  You might want to upgrade your shell
   to a newer version.

   Another reason is problems with gpm.  Try the "--nomouse" option to
   see if it makes any difference.  Restarting gpm can help.  Sometimes
   it's enough to move the mouse.

   Also, if you have the DISPLAY environment variable set, but the X
   server is unreachable (e.g. it's firewalled), this can also cause a
   delay at startup.  Unset DISPLAY to see if that's the case.


11 Development

11.1 Who has written this?

   M-Commander is maintained by Ilia Maslakov.  It is a fork of GNU
   Midnight Commander, which was started by Miguel de Icaza and written
   by many people since 1994; the AUTHORS file lists them all.

11.2 Do I dare to use a development version?

   Yes, of course.  The master branch of

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander

   is where the work happens; check it out and build it as the INSTALL
   file describes.

   But always remember: development versions may have nasty bugs at some
   points of time.  It's up to you to judge whether the new features and
   fixes for the bugs you know outweigh the risk of unknown bugs.

11.3 How can I report a bug/request for a feature?

   You might first want to get the newest development version to see if
   the bug is fixed or the feature is added already.

   If this is not the case, open a ticket at

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander/issues

   Bugs of this fork do not belong to the upstream project, and bugs of
   the upstream program do not belong here.

   Give as many details as possible.  A too long message is a lot better
   than a too short message.  Say which version you run ("mc -V"), which
   terminal and which operating system.

   For segmentation faults a stack backtrace is appreciated.  You can
   produce a stack backtrace as follows:
     * If the segmentation fault produced a core file:
         1. Load the core file by typing "gdb mc core".
         2. Type "where".
         3. Cut and paste the results into your report.
     * If the segmentation fault didn't produce a core file:
         1. Load mc by typing "gdb mc".
         2. Start mc by typing "run".
         3. Try to reproduce the segmentation fault by doing whatever
            you did last time when the segmentation fault occurred.
         4. Type "where".
         5. Cut and paste the results into your report.
         6. For the future you might want to check out what the command
            in your shell is to allow producing of core files.  Usually
            it is "ulimit -c unlimited".

11.4 How can I join the development?

   Send a pull request on GitHub, or open a ticket with the patch.
   Before you start coding, check the latest development version; it
   might be that your feature has already been implemented.  The HACKING
   file describes how the source is laid out and how it is formatted.

   The code you write stays yours.  Unlike the upstream project, this
   fork asks for no copyright assignment.

11.5 How can I help with translations?

   The program strings are translated on Codeberg Weblate:

     https://translate.codeberg.org/engage/mc6/

   The hints in doc/hints and the manual pages in doc/man are not on
   Weblate; send them as a pull request.


12 More information

12.1 This document didn't answer my question.  Where else can I look
for an answer?

   Press F1: the built-in help is the manual page, and it covers nearly
   everything.  The wiki has the longer guides:

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander/wiki

   Upgrade to a newer version.  Many problems are fixed in the new
   versions.

   If you still can't find an answer, ask in a ticket at
   https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander/issues .

12.2 Where should I look on the World Wide Web?

   The project lives at

     https://github.com/blue-panels/mcommander

   The program it comes from is at https://midnight-commander.org .


